  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/sciades_parkeri.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Habitat: brackish, marine</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/sciades_parkeri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Western Atlantic: from the Gulf of Paria, Venezuela to Maranhão State, Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/sciades_parkeri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Mainly found in turbid waters over muddy bottoms in coastal areas, estuaries and lower parts of rivers. Sexually mature after around two years of age. Upon hatching, the alevins are relatively large, approximately 6.0–6.5 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/sciades_parkeri.htm",
      "html": "<p>Grows too large for the home aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/sciades_parkeri.htm",
      "html": "<p>The classification of species within the Ariidae is arguably the most poorly resolved of any catfish family.</p><p>Description: dorsal spines (total) 1; dorsal soft rays (total) 7; anal soft rays 18–20; vertebrae 49–51. In life, the body is yellowish; the swim bladder is divided into 3 chambers; the nuchal plate is shield-shaped, usually larger than the supraoccipital process.</p><p>IUCN Red List: Vulnerable (A2bd). The species is widely distributed, common and abundant in estuaries and lower reaches of rivers, and is commercially exploited for its meat and swim bladder. Off Brazil, landings declined by 47–54% between 1997 and 2007.</p><p>Etymology: the specific name parkeri honours Charles Stewart Parker (1800–1868), British merchant and friend of Traill.</p>"
    }
  ],
